WEBVTT

00:00.300 --> 00:02.746
Marina Beach is hundreds of meters wide.

00:02.746 --> 00:06.564
A few kilometers to the north, there is almost no beach at all,

00:06.564 --> 00:08.950
and the shore is lined with rock walls.

00:08.950 --> 00:12.291
It is the same coast, the same sea, and the same waves.

00:12.291 --> 00:15.154
To explain the difference you need three ideas:

00:15.154 --> 00:19.211
how waves work, how waves move sand along a coast, and what happens

00:19.211 --> 00:21.120
when something gets in the way.

00:21.120 --> 00:23.794
Waves are made by wind blowing across the sea.

00:23.794 --> 00:26.945
The stronger the wind, the longer it blows, and the more open

00:26.945 --> 00:29.091
water it crosses, the bigger the waves.

00:29.091 --> 00:32.724
In deep water, a wave moves forward, but the water itself mostly

00:32.724 --> 00:35.130
moves in circles and stays where it is.

00:35.130 --> 00:37.540
It is the energy that travels, not the water.

00:37.540 --> 00:39.205
Near the shore, that changes.

00:39.205 --> 00:42.531
The bottom of the wave drags on the seabed and slows down.

00:42.531 --> 00:43.967
The top keeps going.

00:43.967 --> 00:47.787
The wave gets taller and steeper until it falls forward and breaks.

00:47.787 --> 00:50.363
That is where the energy is finally used:

00:50.363 --> 00:52.521
moving sand and wearing away rock.

00:52.521 --> 00:55.527
Waves almost never arrive exactly straight on.

00:55.527 --> 00:57.613
They usually come in at an angle.

00:57.613 --> 01:01.070
When a wave breaks, the water rushes up the beach at that angle.

01:01.070 --> 01:04.486
This is called the swash, and it carries sand with it.

01:04.486 --> 01:08.078
Then the water runs straight back down the slope, pulled by gravity.

01:08.078 --> 01:09.807
This is called the backwash.

01:09.807 --> 01:12.518
So each grain of sand moves in a zigzag:

01:12.518 --> 01:16.317
up the beach at an angle, straight back down, again and again.

01:16.317 --> 01:18.998
Each wave moves it a little farther along.

01:18.998 --> 01:22.845
Add up millions of waves, and you get a slow river of sand flowing

01:22.845 --> 01:25.886
along the coast. This is called longshore drift.

01:25.886 --> 01:30.484
On the Chennai coast, over a whole year, more sand moves north

01:30.484 --> 01:33.534
than south. Waves build two families of landforms.

01:33.534 --> 01:37.620
Where the energy is high and the coast is rock, you get erosion:

01:37.620 --> 01:41.097
cliffs, flat wave-cut platforms, and sea stacks.

01:41.097 --> 01:44.665
Rocky headlands take the most energy, because waves bend toward

01:44.665 --> 01:47.905
them. Where the energy is lower and there is a supply of sand,

01:47.905 --> 01:52.362
you get deposition: beaches, spits, which are ridges of sand growing

01:52.362 --> 01:55.073
along the coast, and bars across river mouths.

01:55.073 --> 01:56.988
One idea is worth remembering.

01:56.988 --> 01:59.403
A beach is not a permanent pile of sand.

01:59.403 --> 02:00.850
It is sand in transit.

02:00.850 --> 02:04.471
It stays the same size only as long as the sand arriving matches

02:04.471 --> 02:07.766
the sand leaving. Now build a wall out from the shore, across

02:07.766 --> 02:11.428
the drift. Sand arriving from one side cannot get past.

02:11.428 --> 02:15.380
It piles up against the wall, and the beach on that side grows.

02:15.380 --> 02:19.970
On the far side, waves keep carrying sand away, but no new sand

02:19.970 --> 02:21.477
arrives to replace it.

02:21.477 --> 02:24.070
That beach shrinks, and the shore erodes.

02:24.070 --> 02:27.730
Here is the rule. The beach grows on the side the drift comes

02:27.730 --> 02:29.816
from, and it erodes on the far side.

02:29.816 --> 02:34.085
This works for a harbor wall, for a groyne, and for any structure

02:34.085 --> 02:36.085
built out from a sandy coast.

02:36.085 --> 02:39.385
This is Chennai seen from space, with north at the top.

02:39.385 --> 02:40.794
The port is in the middle.

02:40.794 --> 02:43.901
Its harbor walls were first built in the eighteen seventies and

02:43.901 --> 02:45.965
eighties. The drift comes from the south.

02:45.965 --> 02:49.821
So south of the port, sand has been trapped for more than a century.

02:49.821 --> 02:51.167
That is Marina Beach.

02:51.167 --> 02:53.981
North of the port, the sand supply was cut off.

02:53.981 --> 02:57.605
The shore there has eroded badly, and a long line of rock walls

02:57.605 --> 03:00.044
and groynes has been built to protect the neighborhoods behind

03:00.044 --> 03:02.925
it. Marina Beach, in part, is a result of the harbor.

03:02.925 --> 03:05.565
A coast does not change steadily.

03:05.565 --> 03:08.584
On most days, small waves move a little sand.

03:08.584 --> 03:13.209
During a cyclone or a strong monsoon storm, large waves can strip

03:13.209 --> 03:16.417
a beach in a single day and move the sand offshore.

03:16.417 --> 03:20.526
In calmer months, gentle waves slowly bring it back, and the beach

03:20.526 --> 03:24.606
widens again. On this coast, the northeast monsoon, from October

03:24.606 --> 03:28.489
to December, brings the biggest waves and most of the cyclones.

03:28.489 --> 03:30.982
So here is a warning for your own work.

03:30.982 --> 03:34.353
When you compare two satellite images of a beach, check the month

03:34.353 --> 03:38.528
of each one first. A narrower beach might be a seasonal change,

03:38.528 --> 03:39.829
not a permanent one.

03:39.829 --> 03:42.121
Check yourself with three questions.

03:42.121 --> 03:43.484
Pause if you need to.

03:43.484 --> 03:45.033
One. Swash and backwash.

03:45.033 --> 03:47.511
Which one runs straight down the beach?

03:47.511 --> 03:51.353
Two. Drift moves sand north, and a wall is built out from the

03:51.353 --> 03:53.211
shore. Which side gains sand?

03:53.211 --> 03:56.867
Three. A beach is narrower in December than it was in May.

03:56.867 --> 03:58.353
Is it eroding for good?

03:58.353 --> 03:59.716
Here are the answers.

03:59.716 --> 04:02.938
The backwash runs straight down, pulled by gravity.

04:02.938 --> 04:06.779
The south side gains sand, because that is the side the drift

04:06.779 --> 04:10.125
comes from. And you cannot tell from those two dates.

04:10.125 --> 04:13.656
December is storm season, so the change may be seasonal.

04:13.656 --> 04:15.329
Now go on to today's task.
